@meta
id: 14-14
title: Scenario — the rename ticket
chapter: 14
type: scenario
skills: sub-basic, sub-range, sub-confirm, search-slash
par: 166
minutes: 14
vscode: a project-wide Replace All, then a revert, then doing it by hand
open: order.entity.ts order.provider.ts order.test.ts order.types.ts

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    ORD-527  Rename Order.cancellationReason to cancelReason. The
             GraphQL input type CancelOrderInput keeps its field name
             until the mobile app ships — that is a public contract.

Four files, open as four buffers. `:bn` moves to the next one. Write
each file with `:w` before you leave it, and finish with `:wqa`,
because `:wq` will refuse while other files are still open.

  1. order.entity.ts — the field on `Order` and the helper below it.
     Everything here changes.

  2. order.provider.ts — the entity field changes wherever it is read
     or written. The provider also reads the field *from the input*,
     and the input is the contract. Read each hit.

  3. order.test.ts — the assertions and the factory build an entity;
     the calls to `provider.cancel` build an input. Same rule.

  4. order.types.ts — the input type. It is open so you can see the
     contract. Do not change it; the check fails if you do.

The old name never appears inside a longer identifier, so no anchors
are needed. What is needed is judgement on every hit in files 2 and 3.

@keys
| `:%s/old/new/g` `Enter` | whole file, no questions — for the file where every hit changes |
| `:%s/old/new/gc` `Enter` | whole file, ask each time — for the files where some do not |
| `y` / `n` | take it / leave it |
| `:w` then `:bn` | save this buffer, move to the next |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
Read the ticket's exception before you touch a key: the *input* keeps
the old name. An input is anything passed to `cancel` or declared in
`CancelOrderInput`. An entity is what comes back from the repository
or the factory.
--
File 1 has no inputs in it, so `:%s` with no confirm is honest there.
Files 2 and 3 each have both, and one line in the provider has one of
each — the left side of `cancellationReason: input.cancellationReason`
is the entity, the right side is the input.
--
Provider: `:%s/cancellationReason/cancelReason/gc`, answers
`y y n y y`. Test: the same command, answers `n y y n`. Then `:wqa`.

@solution
**order.entity.ts** — `:%s/cancellationReason/cancelReason/g`, `:w`,
`:bn`.

**order.provider.ts** — `:%s/cancellationReason/cancelReason/gc`, and
read: the guard is entity (`y`), the update values line is entity then
input (`y`, `n`), the filter and the map are entity (`y`, `y`). `:w`,
`:bn`.

**order.test.ts** — the same command. The first hit is an argument to
`provider.cancel` (`n`), the assertion is entity (`y`), the factory
builds an entity (`y`), the second `cancel` call is input (`n`). Then
`:wqa` writes the two you changed, leaves the types file alone, and
quits.

About a hundred and forty keystrokes, nine of them decisions, and the
decisions are the job. Every one of the substitutes was something you
already knew; the ticket was a test of whether you would stop and read
the exception in it, or replace-all and hope. That single line in the
provider, entity on the left and input on the right, is the whole
argument for the `c` flag: no range and no pattern separates two
identical words on one line by what they mean.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'PAID' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

export function isCancelled(order: Order) {
  return order.status === 'CANCELLED' && order.cancellationReason !== undefined;
}

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancelReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'PAID' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

export function isCancelled(order: Order) {
  return order.status === 'CANCELLED' && order.cancelReason !== undefined;
}

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CancelOrderInput } from './order.types';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(@inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async cancel(input: CancelOrderInput) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: input.id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.cancellationReason) {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: input.id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED', cancellationReason: input.cancellationReason },
    });
  }

  public async cancellationReport(customerId: string) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });

    return orders
      .filter((order) => order.cancellationReason !== undefined)
      .map((order) => ({ id: order.id, reason: order.cancellationReason }));
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CancelOrderInput } from './order.types';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(@inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async cancel(input: CancelOrderInput) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: input.id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.cancelReason) {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: input.id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED', cancelReason: input.cancellationReason },
    });
  }

  public async cancellationReport(customerId: string) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });

    return orders
      .filter((order) => order.cancelReason !== undefined)
      .map((order) => ({ id: order.id, reason: order.cancelReason }));
  }
}

@start order.test.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { orderFactory } from '../../test/factories';

describe('OrderProvider.cancel', () => {
  it('records the reason on the order', async () => {
    const order = await provider.cancel({ id: 'ord_8101', cancellationReason: 'DAMAGED' });

    expect(order.status).toEqual('CANCELLED');
    expect(order.cancellationReason).toEqual('DAMAGED');
  });

  it('refuses to cancel twice', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory.build({ cancellationReason: 'DAMAGED' });

    await expect(provider.cancel({ id: order.id, cancellationReason: 'CHANGED_MIND' })).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED');
  });
});

@expected order.test.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { orderFactory } from '../../test/factories';

describe('OrderProvider.cancel', () => {
  it('records the reason on the order', async () => {
    const order = await provider.cancel({ id: 'ord_8101', cancellationReason: 'DAMAGED' });

    expect(order.status).toEqual('CANCELLED');
    expect(order.cancelReason).toEqual('DAMAGED');
  });

  it('refuses to cancel twice', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory.build({ cancelReason: 'DAMAGED' });

    await expect(provider.cancel({ id: order.id, cancellationReason: 'CHANGED_MIND' })).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED');
  });
});

@start order.types.ts
// Public GraphQL input. Field names are a contract with the mobile app.
export type CancelOrderInput = {
  id: string;
  cancellationReason: string;
};
